From: Eduardo Ruiz <335073+eduarbo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 08:44:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Show the system grid in tabs switched with L1 and R1

A system grid shows every visible system in one long list. On a
640x480 handheld with three dozen systems, a home split by era (8 bits,
16 bits, portables, arcade...), one tab at a time with L1/R1 between
tabs, is easier to browse; today L1/R1 only page through the grid.

Add tabs to the system grid, defined by the theme variable
"systemtabs": "NAME=system,system;NAME=...", in display order. Each
visible system goes to the first tab that lists it; unlisted ones go to
the tab marked "*" (else the last tab), so a new system is never lost.
The grid then holds only the shown tab's systems. L1/R1 move to the
previous or next tab that has systems (wrapping) and come back to the
system last selected there. goToSystem shows the system's tab first, so
going back from a game list or starting on a system lands right.

{system:tab} gives the index of a system's tab, so a theme can draw the
tab row. A tab with more systems than the grid's autoLayout shows uses
<imagegrid name="imagegrid-dense"> when the theme defines one.

ES versions with this support the theme feature "systemtabs", so a
theme can keep its own system view for other versions inside
<feature supported="systemtabs">. Such an include may redefine the
items of a theme's grid with <itemTemplate replace="true">: it drops
the element's earlier templates (the grid's tiles take the first one).
Without the attribute nothing changes: the first template stays.

ThemeElement had a deep copy constructor and a destructor that deletes
its storyboards, but the implicit copy assignment, which shared the
storyboard pointers. Give it a copy-and-swap assignment, and rebuild the
children by copy when templates are dropped.

Without the variable, or with a carousel or text list, nothing changes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
